{
  "$comment": [
    "Block table for Minecraft Java Alpha v1.1.2 / v1.1.2_01.",
    "This file is checked in and compiled into the binary. The game ships",
    "knowing all of it -- players never extract or supply block data.",
    "Every numeric column was recovered once, by a maintainer, from an original",
    "client jar by tools/extract_blocks.py, and can be re-verified with",
    "--verify. The `name` column is ours: the alpha-era Block class carries no",
    "names at all. See docs/world-format.md and CONTRIBUTING.md.",
    "`texture` is the block's own atlas tile. `faces` is present only when the",
    "six sides differ, in the game's face order (0 -Y, 1 +Y, 2 -Z, 3 +Z, 4 -X,",
    "5 +X); when it is absent every face uses `texture`. Face textures that",
    "vary with metadata or with the surrounding world hold the no-metadata,",
    "no-neighbours answer -- run the extractor to have it name them. A negative",
    "value is the original's flag for a horizontally mirrored face (doors).",
    "`metadataFaces` is present only for a block whose in-world faces follow its",
    "own metadata and nothing else -- the furnace, whose mouth is the side the",
    "metadata names. Sixteen rows of six, indexed by metadata; `faces` stays the",
    "inventory answer, which puts the mouth on +Z.",
    "`shape` is the collision shape, generated by classifying tests/collision_box_vectors.hpp -- see src/core/block/collision.hpp.",
    "`slipperiness` is Block.slipperiness, the ground friction EntityLiving.moveEntityWithHeading multiplies by 0.91. 0.6 everywhere but ice.",
    "`targetable` is Block.canCollideCheck -- isCollidable() in a1.1.2 -- which decides whether a ray notices the block at all. False only for water, lava and fire.",
    "`tick` names the block's behaviour. The two signs get one name each and so do the two pressure plates, rather than sharing one apiece: a sign post asks what is under it and a wall sign asks the face its metadata names, and `lr` tells them apart by a boolean constructor argument this table has nowhere else to put. Likewise for the plates: `al`'s third constructor argument is a `js` (EnumMobType), and the jar passes `js.b` (\"mobs\") for block 70 and `js.a` (\"everything\") for block 72 -- so a dropped item presses the wooden plate and not the stone one.",
    "`contact` is what `ly.b(Lcn;IIILkh;)V` -- onEntityCollidedWithBlock -- does to an entity whose box overlaps the block, called from the tail of `kh.c(DDD)V` for every cell it touches. Two classes override it in a1.1.2: `hy`, the cactus, whose whole body is attackEntityFrom(null, 1), and `al`, both pressure plates. Absent means the base class, which does nothing. See src/core/block/block_def.hpp.",
    "`stepSound` names one of `stepSounds`, by the field letter Block stores the singleton in -- the same shape `material` uses. `step` is what walking on the block plays and what *placing* it plays; `break` is what breaking it plays, and the two differ only for sand (which breaks like gravel) and glass.",
    "`model` is the block a block is built from (a Block-typed constructor argument): a staircase's planks or cobblestone, which it turns into with something solid on top of it.",
    "`worldTexture` names the rule a block's faces follow when the tile depends on a *neighbouring* cell rather than on the block itself -- the one thing `faces` cannot carry, since it is a table and this is a question about the world. It is ours and assigned by hand, as `tick` is: the extractor can only report which faces need the world (`worldDependentFaces`), because the rule is a branch and not a value. The extractor names four ids (2, 53, 54, 67); the two staircases are there only because they forward to a model block whose world overload reads the metadata and then ignores it, so the blocks that genuinely read a *neighbour* are grass and the chest, and the chest is the one that is ported -- `b.a(Lnm;IIII)I` turns its front away from the blocks around it and joins a neighbouring chest into one two-cell picture. See src/core/block/world_texture.hpp."
  ],
